{"name":"Milk Tooth","hex":"#faebd7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"milk tooth","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any tooth of the first set of teeth of young people (or animals)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/milk_tooth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Deciduous teeth","blocks":[{"text":"Deciduous teeth or primary teeth, also informally known as baby teeth, milk teeth, or temporary teeth, are the first set of teeth in the growth and development of humans and other diphyodonts, which include most mammals but not elephants, kangaroos, or manatees, which are polyphyodonts. Deciduous teeth develop during the embryonic stage of development and erupt during infancy. They are usually lost and replaced by permanent teeth, but in the absence of their permanent replacements, they can remain functional for many years into adulthood."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b8/Gray1001.png/330px-Gray1001.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deciduous_teeth","wikidata":"Q281524"},{"title":"Milk","blocks":[{"text":"Milk is usually a white liquid food produced by the mammary glands of lactating mammals. It is the primary source of nutrition for young mammals before they are able to digest solid food. Milk contains many nutrients, including calcium and protein, as well as lactose and saturated fat; the enzyme lactase is needed to break down lactose. Immune factors and immune-modulating components in milk contribute to milk immunity. The first milk, which is called colostrum, contains antibodies and immune-modulating components that strengthen the immune system against many diseases."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Glass_of_Milk_%2833657535532%29.jpg/330px-Glass_of_Milk_%2833657535532%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milk","wikidata":"Q8495"},{"title":"Tooth","blocks":[{"text":"A tooth is a hard, calcified structure found in the jaws of many vertebrates and used to break down food. Some animals, particularly carnivores and omnivores, also use teeth to help with capturing or wounding prey, tearing food, for defensive purposes, to intimidate other animals often including their own, or to carry prey or their young. The roots of teeth are covered by gums. Teeth are not made of bone, but rather of multiple tissues of varying density and hardness that originate from the outermost embryonic germ layer, the ectoderm."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/Close_up_-_chimpanzee_teeth.png/330px-Close_up_-_chimpanzee_teeth.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tooth","wikidata":"Q553"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/ToothLost-2917.jpg/500px-ToothLost-2917.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/ToothLost-2917.jpg/1280px-ToothLost-2917.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/ToothLost-2917.jpg/500px-ToothLost-2917.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bottom teeth of a seven-year-old, showing primary teeth (left), a lost primary tooth and visible socket (middle), and a fully erupted permanent tooth (right)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AToothLost-2917.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Melkkarussell.jpg/500px-Melkkarussell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Melkkarussell.jpg/1280px-Melkkarussell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/Melkkarussell.jpg/500px-Melkkarussell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cows in a rotary milking parlor","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMelkkarussell.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Woman_breastfeeding_an_infant.jpg/500px-Woman_breastfeeding_an_infant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Woman_breastfeeding_an_infant.jpg/500px-Woman_breastfeeding_an_infant.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Breastfeeding to provide a mother's milk","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWoman_breastfeeding_an_infant.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Goat_kid_feeding_on_mothers_milk.jpg/500px-Goat_kid_feeding_on_mothers_milk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Goat_kid_feeding_on_mothers_milk.jpg/1280px-Goat_kid_feeding_on_mothers_milk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Goat_kid_feeding_on_mothers_milk.jpg/500px-Goat_kid_feeding_on_mothers_milk.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A goat kid feeding on its mother's milk","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGoat_kid_feeding_on_mothers_milk.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Cow_female_black_white.jpg/500px-Cow_female_black_white.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0c/Cow_female_black_white.jpg/1280px-Cow_female_black_white.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&ut